think The input family's four floors all stand GREEN and sent (line, history, shell, prompt), and scooter_view draws the journal. The honest capstone crux is the full terminal -- composing the journal above and the drawn prompt below into ONE screen, the shape of a chat client at the command line. Pure composition, ungated, finishable green in one lap.
think The clean composition needs the journal to know it has one fewer row (the prompt owns the last row). Rather than overwrite scooter_view's overflow tail, I additively generalized scooter_view.render into render_within(ch, body_avail) -- render now calls render_within(ch, body_rows), unchanged behavior, and its own witness re-runs GREEN. dexter_terminal calls render_within(ch, journal_body) with journal_body = term_rows - 2.
think Built pond/apps/dexter_terminal.rye: render_terminal draws the journal into rows 0..prompt_row-1 and dexter_prompt's row into the last row, tracking the caret's absolute place (its row and column). The crux is disjointness -- the journal writes only the rows above, the prompt only the last row, so even a full journal leaves the last row for the prompt (the overflow tail lands on the row just above, never on the prompt row).
obs GREEN on the first metal run -- no red. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, new prose plain ASCII. The witness build compiled the unchanged scooter_channel, dexter_line, and dexter_prompt; scooter_view changed additively and its witness re-runs GREEN (behavior-preserving).
obs This ties to the itinerary (rota item 26): the loop climbs faster than the calendar names the rungs -- the good problem. The terminal family is a Season G rung the double-seat named, and this arc filled five agent-doable rounds (line, history, shell, prompt, terminal) as its own self-approved quest, each witnessed and sent. Only the live keystroke reader and the wire between piers remain -- both at the serve gate.
obs Rota lap (d27, item 26) read active-designing/20260812-171050_the-1024-round-itinerary.md -- Lindy-first crux-first orders the road; a quest is filled not invented; the gates are the fence.
